/**

\page page_continuous_integration Continuous Integration (CI)

\tableofcontents

## Gitlab CI

[The GitLab documentation](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/) describes the continuous integration of GitLab. Peano uses it as
follows:

\li Every push to a branch without an open merge request and every merge request start a pipeline of the jobs in
    `.gitlab-ci.yml`.
\li `.gitlab-ci.yml` includes the files in `.gitlab`. The stage `Python` compiles the Python sources and checks the import
    layers of the Python API (`python.yml`). The stage `CPU` runs one job per compiler toolchain (`cpu.yml`). The stage `GPU`
    runs one job per GPU toolchain (`gpu.yml`).
\li The matrix of such a job lists the combinations of the build mode `BUILD_MODE` and the space-filling curve
    `SPACE_FILLING_CURVE` that it covers. Every combination triggers a child pipeline from `.gitlab/pipeline.yml` with the name
    of its trigger job, for example `GCC: [Debug, Hilbert]`.
\li Every job runs the build modes `Release`, `Debug` and `Asserts` on the curve of the template `.Peano` or `.Hilbert` that it
    extends. GCC runs on the Hilbert curve. LLVM, Intel, NVIDIA, AMD and every GPU job run on the Peano curve.
\li LLVM and GCC set `SANITIZER` to `address+undefined`. Their `Asserts` pipelines then build with AddressSanitizer and
    UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
\li The child pipeline includes the templates in `templates.yml` and the jobs of the stages `Build`, `Test` and `Application` in
    `build.yml`, `test.yml` and `application.yml`.
\li The runners run without a GPU. `.GPU` sets `RUN_UNIT_TESTS` and `RUN_SIMULATIONS` to `false`. A GPU child pipeline only
    builds the libraries, the unit tests, the tests and the applications for `sm_75`.
\li A GPU child pipeline skips the API test, the Fortran coupling and the tutorials. Their PDE terms run on the host only. A GPU
    build rejects such terms. The CPU child pipelines build them with the same compilers.
\li With both variables set to `true` in `.GPU`, a GPU child pipeline runs everything that a CPU child pipeline runs.
\li Every job runs its commands in an apptainer image of `APPTAINER_IMAGEDIR`. Every runner then builds with the same software.
\li The job `Build and Unit Tests` of a child pipeline compiles the libraries and the unit tests of its build mode through
    ccache and runs these unit tests. The GitLab cache of the runner keeps the ccache objects between pipelines, one cache per
    child pipeline.
\li The tests, tutorials and applications run in the build mode of their child pipeline.
\li Every simulation of a test or an application runs `NUMBER_OF_TIME_STEPS` time steps, writes `NUMBER_OF_SNAPSHOTS`
    snapshots and uses the grid levels from `MIN_GRID_LEVELS` to `MAX_GRID_LEVELS`. `templates.yml` sets these values for the
    whole pipeline and the grid levels once per curve. `pipeline.yml` selects the grid levels of the curve of its child
    pipeline.
\li A job clones the Git LFS files as pointers. An application job pulls the files of its scenario with `git lfs pull`.
\li LRZ GitLab limits the unauthenticated requests per IP address. All runners send their requests from one address. GitLab
    counts a fetch with the CI job token as unauthenticated. The runners fetch the sources with a personal access token (see
    Clone token below).

## How do I run the pipeline?

Every child pipeline builds the libraries and the unit tests. A CPU child pipeline also runs the unit tests. The jobs that
extend `.RunOnAnyBranch` run on every branch whose name starts with `muc/`. The jobs that extend `.RunOnlyOnMainBranch` run on `muc/exahype` and in merge requests.

`gitlab-runner` runs on several nodes of DINE. Recurring Slurm jobs run these runners. A developer pushes to a branch and
checks on the [pipelines page](https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano/-/pipelines) that the code builds with every compiler
toolchain.

When the Slurm job of a runner ends during a pipeline, the jobs of this runner hang until they time out. The developer then
restarts the pipeline on the pipelines page.

## How do I write my own tests?

Copy a job of `.gitlab/test.yml` and adapt it:

```
My Test:
  stage: Test
  parallel:
    matrix:
      - scenario: [first-scenario, second-scenario]
  script:
    - generate tests/my-test "$scenario.py --build-mode $BUILD_MODE"
    - launch tests/my-test "./ExaHyPE -nt ${NUMBER_OF_TIME_STEPS} -ns ${NUMBER_OF_SNAPSHOTS} -lmin ${MIN_GRID_LEVELS} -lmax ${MAX_GRID_LEVELS}"
  extends: .RunOnAnyBranch
  needs: [Build and Unit Tests]
```

The job runs after the job `Build and Unit Tests` of its child pipeline and reads the libraries that this job installed. Every
script line runs on the runner from the top level of the Peano checkout. The shell functions of `.gitlab/templates.yml` run a
command inside the container of the job:

\li `in_image "command"` runs the command in the container.
\li `generate directory "arguments"` runs `python3` with the arguments in the directory and the Python API on the
    `PYTHONPATH`.
\li `launch directory "executable and arguments"` runs the executable in the directory through `PARALLEL_LAUNCH`.
    `PARALLEL_LAUNCH` starts `MPI_RANKS` ranks with `OMP_THREADS` threads each.
\li `check_error_norms directory file tolerance` compares the error norms in the file with the tolerance.

In a pipeline whose `RUN_SIMULATIONS` is `false`, `launch` and `check_error_norms` skip the run and the check. The job only
builds the executable. A tutorial runs the cells of its notebook up to the build (`extract_build_cells.py`).
`.RunOnAnyBranch` runs the job on every branch whose name starts with `muc/`. `.RunOnlyOnMainBranch` runs the job on
`muc/exahype` and in merge requests.

## How do I add new runners to the project?

More runners run more jobs at once. Only the project owner adds a runner:

\li Open Settings > CI/CD > Runners.
\li Add a new project runner with the tag `peano-dine-runner`. Every job of the repository requests this tag.
\li Lock the runner to the current project and click create.

GitLab then shows a token. Write it down.

Log into a node with `gitlab-runner` and run `gitlab-runner register`. Type in the URL and the token as prompted. Pick `shell`
as the executor. The registration writes a configuration file into `$HOME/.gitlab-runner`. Move this file into
`/dine/data/do009/peano/configs`.

`gitlab-runner run --config /path/to/config.toml` runs the runner with this configuration. The Slurm job of a runner runs this
command.

### Config template

\verbatim

concurrent = 4
check_interval = 0
shutdown_timeout = 0

[session_server]
  session_timeout = 1800

[[runners]]
  name = "b101Runner"
  url = "https://gitlab.lrz.de"
  id = 35483
  token = "my-token"
  token_obtained_at = 2024-06-19T11:16:06Z
  token_expires_at = 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z
  executor = "shell"
  builds_dir = "/dine/data/do009/peano/builds_dir"
  cache_dir  = "/dine/data/do009/peano/cache_dir"
  pre_get_sources_script = "if [ -r /dine/data/do009/peano/configs/clone-token.sh ]; then . /dine/data/do009/peano/configs/clone-token.sh; fi"

\endverbatim

`concurrent` sets the number of jobs that the runner runs at once.

### Clone token

LRZ GitLab answers unauthenticated requests beyond its rate limit with HTTP 429 until the current window of 30 minutes ends. A
fetch with the CI job token counts as unauthenticated. A fetch with a personal access token counts as authenticated. Every
runner sources `/dine/data/do009/peano/configs/clone-token.sh` before it fetches the sources:

\verbatim

if [ "${GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE:-0}" = "1" ] && [ "$(date +%F)" \< "2027-10-10" ]; then
  { set +x; } 2>/dev/null
  export GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1
  export GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0="http.https://gitlab.lrz.de/.extraHeader"
  export GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0="Authorization: Basic BASE64_OF_USERNAME_AND_TOKEN"
fi

\endverbatim

The token is a personal access token with the scope `read_repository`. `printf 'username:token' | base64 -w 0` gives
`BASE64_OF_USERNAME_AND_TOKEN`. The date is the expiry date of the token. From then on the runners fetch without the token. A
fetch that downloads Git LFS files also fetches without the token. Every job of the runners can read the file. The token
therefore gets the scope `read_repository` only. The variables need Git 2.31 or newer on the runners.

*/
